{
  "slug": "rdi-halcyon",
  "title": "RDI Halcyon",
  "year": "1985",
  "subtitle": "The voice-controlled laserdisc game console that trained itself to your voice, cost $2,500, and sold perhaps ten units before vanishing into legend.",
  "why_scout_cares": "A consumer device in 1985 that bet its entire identity on speech recognition as the primary input mechanism. No joystick, no keyboard, no buttons — you talked to it through a noise-canceling headset, and it talked back. The Halcyon trained itself to your voice before each session, scored its own recognition confidence, and responded with synthesized speech confirmation. The games were interactive laserdisc movies — full-motion video adventures where your spoken commands drove the branching narrative. 'Halcyon, I choose to go left.' This was a decade before the web made voice-control fantasies like 'Knowledge Navigator' famous, and it was real hardware at CES 1985. Only a handful of units were ever produced at $2,500 each, making the Halcyon one of the rarest commercial video game consoles ever made. It is the ghost of a future that arrived 30 years late — when Alexa and Siri finally made voice-first interaction commonplace. The Halcyon tried it with a Z80 CPU, 64K of RAM, and a laserdisc player the size of a suitcase.",
